[38;2;124;124;124m**Infinite Time Turing Machines (ITTMs)** were introduced by Hamkins and Lewis. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mAn ITTM can run for transfinite time. The key ordinals associated with ITTMs:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- λ: the supremum of writable ordinals (ordinals that can be written on the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moutput tape by an ITTM in countable time)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- ζ: the supremum of eventually writable ordinals[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Σ: the supremum of accidentally writable ordinals[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThese satisfy λ < ζ < Σ, and they are all countable ordinals. Moreover, λ is the[0m[38;2;124;124;124msupremum of clockable ordinals (ordinals at which an ITTM can halt).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Iterated ITTMs**: This refers to the hierarchy where we allow ITTMs to use [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moracles. The idea is that we can iterate the "writable" operation. Let me think [0m[38;2;124;124;124mabout what "iterated ITTMs" means precisely.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think about this more carefully. The "writable ordinals" for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mITTMs give λ. If we allow the ITTM to have an oracle for λ (or for the set of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwritable reals), we get a higher ordinal. Iterating this process gives a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhierarchy.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the key concept here is the notion of "eventually writable" and the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124miteration of the jump. Let me recall the work of Welch and others.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe ordinal λ (supremum of writable ordinals) is equal to the supremum of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclockable ordinals. It's known that:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- λ is the least ordinal such that L_λ is admissible... no wait, that's not [0m
